# Above the mean

Read an integer `n` (1 ≤ n ≤ 2000), then `n` integers `a_1 … a_n` (−10^9 ≤ a_i ≤ 10^9),
separated by spaces or newlines. Print how many of them are strictly greater than the mean of
all `n` numbers.

## Example

Input:

```
4
1 2 3 10
```

Output:

```
1
```

The mean is 4, and only 10 is greater than it.

Time limit 1 second, memory limit 256 MB.

</problem_statement>

<constraints>
{"id":"C2","text":"Every recursive function that walks the numbers (reading, summing or counting them) must split its range into two parts of about equal size and recurse on both, so the recursion depth grows with log n, not with n. A recursion that peels off one element, or a constant number of elements, per call does not satisfy this constraint, however the split is written. This constraint is about the shape of the recursion only; loops are covered by C1."}
</constraints>

<submission language="cpp" total_lines="22">
